{
  "outcome": "needs_user_info",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Leo, I'm sorry this has dragged on, and I know it's frustrating to have to ask again. I've found the cause: your laptop's drive is almost full. Only about 9 GB of its 256 GB is free (3.5%), and when it gets that low, files can't save. Here's how to free up space, in this order:\n1) Empty the Recycle Bin, then delete anything you don't need in your Downloads folder.\n2) Go to Settings > System > Storage > Temporary files. Select temporary files and old Windows Update files, then click Remove.\n3) Turn on OneDrive Files On-Demand: right-click the OneDrive cloud icon, then go to Settings > Sync and back up > Advanced settings > Files On-Demand. Your synced files will stay in the cloud until you open them.\n4) Uninstall any apps you no longer use (Settings > Apps > Installed apps).\nThe goal is to get to at least 10% free, which is about 26 GB. Please reply once you've done these steps. I'll check the laptop again from my end. If it's still too full after the cleanup, I'll pass it straight to our senior team to look at a bigger drive or a replacement laptop.",
  "internal_note": "User: Leo Martins (user_id 6, Sales AE). Device BL-LT-0106 (Win 11 Pro) is online and checked in recently. Disk: 9.0 GB free of 256 GB (3.5%), with a low disk space warning. This matches KB-017 (files won't save). Sent the user the KB-017 cleanup steps 1-4. Next: when the user replies, re-run check_device_status. If free space is still under 10%, escalate to Tier 2 for a bigger drive or replacement. The user says this is their third request and is frustrated, but I found no earlier ticket context. Consider escalating sooner if they come back without progress. Account and mailbox look normal (mailbox 26% used).",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-017"
  ],
  "confidence": "high"
}
